from lib import *
S='science'
lesson(10,S,'nuclear-physics','radioactivity-and-nuclear-energy',"Radioactivity, fission, fusion and nuclear energy","A forgotten drawer and a new science","In 1896 Henri Becquerel left uranium salts in a drawer on top of an unexposed photographic plate. When he developed the plate it was fogged, as if exposed to light. Uranium was giving out invisible rays by itself, and the science of radioactivity began.",
["In 1896 Henri Becquerel discovered radioactivity from uranium; the Curies later found it in pitchblende, an ore of uranium. @ discovery",
 "The spontaneous emission of alpha, beta and gamma radiations from certain elements is called radioactivity, and such elements are radioactive. @ radioactivity",
 "Natural radioactivity is spontaneous; elements with atomic number above 82, such as uranium and radium, show it, and only technetium (43) and promethium (61) below 82. @ natural radioactivity",
 "Alpha particles are helium nuclei with +2 charge, beta particles are fast electrons, and gamma rays are electromagnetic waves; gamma penetrates most and alpha least. @ alpha beta gamma",
 "In alpha decay the atomic number falls by 2 and the mass number by 4; in beta decay the atomic number rises by 1 and the mass number is unchanged (Soddy and Fajan's displacement law). @ displacement law",
 "Nuclear fission splits a heavy nucleus such as uranium-235 by a neutron into two lighter nuclei and releases huge energy with more neutrons, which can cause a chain reaction. @ fission",
 "A controlled chain reaction runs a nuclear reactor, while an uncontrolled one gives an atom bomb; nuclear fusion joins light nuclei such as hydrogen into helium, as in the Sun and the hydrogen bomb. @ chain reaction and fusion",
 "Radioactivity is used in medicine, power generation, carbon dating and tracing, but radioactive material needs precautions such as lead shielding and dosimeters. @ uses and safety"],
("Uranium-238 (atomic number 92) emits one alpha particle. What are the atomic number and mass number of the new nucleus?",[("Alpha decay reduces atomic number by 2: 92 - 2 = 90.","Z falls by 2."),("It reduces mass number by 4: 238 - 4 = 234.","A falls by 4."),("The new nucleus is thorium-234.","Element with Z = 90 is thorium.")]),
("Compare alpha, beta and gamma rays and explain fission and fusion.",["Alpha helium nuclei, beta electrons, gamma waves","Penetration order gamma > beta > alpha","Fission splits heavy nuclei, fusion joins light nuclei"],["Believing gamma rays carry charge"]),
["Radioactivity: spontaneous emission of alpha, beta, gamma rays.","Alpha decay: Z-2, A-4; beta decay: Z+1, A same.","Fission splits heavy nuclei; fusion joins light nuclei.","Safety: shielding, limited exposure."],
["m|Radioactivity was discovered by|Henri Becquerel|Rutherford~Found the nucleus;Dalton~Atomic theory|He used uranium and a photographic plate.",
 "t|Gamma rays carry a positive charge.|False|They are uncharged electromagnetic waves.",
 "n|Atomic number after alpha decay of an element with Z=88 (radium)|86|Z falls by 2.",
 "n|Mass number after one alpha decay from 226|222|A falls by 4.",
 "m|Fusion is the process in which|light nuclei combine to form a heavier nucleus|a heavy nucleus splits~That is fission|electrons are lost~Not fusion|It powers the Sun.",
 "t|Uncontrolled chain reaction is the principle of the atom bomb.|True|Energy is released at once.",
 "s|The ore of uranium in which the Curies detected radioactivity is ____.|pitchblende|A tiny black ore.",
 "m|The most penetrating radiation is|gamma rays|alpha particles~Stopped by paper|beta particles~Stopped by thin metal|Gamma passes through much material."])
lesson(10,S,'thermal-physics','temperature-scales-and-thermal-equilibrium',"Temperature, the Kelvin scale and thermal equilibrium","Why does a hot tea cool down?","A cup of hot tea cools and a cold bottle warms until both match the room. Energy flows from hot to cold, never the other way, until they reach the same temperature. That is thermal equilibrium, and the Kelvin scale lets us measure it from absolute zero.",
["Temperature is the degree of hotness of a body, a scalar quantity that decides the direction of heat flow; its SI unit is the kelvin (K). @ temperature",
 "Thermal energy or heat flows from a hot body to a cold body because of the temperature difference, and it is the agent that produces the sensation of warmth. @ heat flow",
 "The Kelvin scale is the absolute or thermodynamic scale: K = C + 273, and zero kelvin equals -273 degrees Celsius. @ Kelvin scale",
 "A difference of 1 degree Celsius equals a difference of 1 K; one kelvin is 1/273.16 of the triple point of water. @ unit size",
 "Two bodies are in thermal equilibrium if there is no net flow of thermal energy between them, which means they are at the same temperature. @ thermal equilibrium",
 "Heat flows until the temperatures of the bodies become equal. @ flow stops",
 "Temperature measures hotness; heat is the energy transferred, so they are not the same thing. @ temperature vs heat"],
("Convert 27 degrees Celsius to kelvin and 300 K to Celsius.",[("Use K = C + 273, so 27 + 273 = 300 K.","Add 273."),("To go back use C = K - 273, so 300 - 273 = 27 degrees Celsius.","Subtract 273.")]),
("Define temperature, heat and thermal equilibrium and state the SI unit of temperature.",["Temperature is degree of hotness","Heat flows from hot to cold","No net flow at equilibrium","Unit is kelvin"],["Using temperature and heat as the same thing"]),
["Temperature: degree of hotness; SI unit kelvin.","K = C + 273; 0 K = -273 degrees Celsius.","Heat flows from higher to lower temperature.","At thermal equilibrium temperatures are equal and net flow stops."],
["n|Convert 27 degrees Celsius to kelvin|300|K = C + 273.",
 "n|Convert 100 degrees Celsius to kelvin|373|100 + 273.",
 "t|Heat flows from a cold body to a hot body.|False|It flows from hot to cold.",
 "m|The SI unit of temperature is|kelvin|degree Celsius~Common unit but not SI|calorie~A unit of heat|K.",
 "t|Two bodies in thermal equilibrium are at the same temperature.|True|There is no net heat flow.",
 "n|0 K in degrees Celsius|-273|Absolute zero.",
 "s|The absolute scale of temperature is also called the ____ scale.|kelvin/Kelvin|It is thermodynamic temperature.",
 "m|Temperature is a|scalar quantity|vector quantity~It has no direction|force~Not a force|It has only magnitude."])
lesson(10,S,'acoustics','echo-doppler-and-frequency-ranges',"Sound waves, echo and the Doppler effect","Shouting into a valley","Shout towards a distant cliff and a moment later your own voice comes back. That is an echo. This lesson ties together sound as a vibration, its frequency ranges and the way echoes measure distance.",
["Sound is produced by vibrations and needs a material medium such as air, water or steel to travel; it cannot travel through a vacuum such as on the Moon. @ sound needs a medium",
 "Audible waves have frequencies between 20 Hz and 20,000 Hz; infrasonic waves are below 20 Hz and ultrasonic waves are above 20 kHz. @ frequency ranges",
 "Infrasonic waves are produced by earthquakes, ocean waves and whales; bats, dogs and dolphins can detect ultrasonic waves. @ examples",
 "Speed of sound v = frequency times wavelength (v = f x lambda), and the speed is different in solids, liquids and gases. @ v = f lambda",
 "Reflection of sound at a hard surface produces an echo; the reflected sound must reach the ear at least 0.1 second after the original to be heard separately. @ echo",
 "Using an echo, distance d = v x t / 2 because the sound travels to the reflector and back. @ echo distance",
 "The Doppler effect is the apparent change in frequency when the source and listener move relative to each other. @ Doppler effect"],
("You shout and hear the echo after 2 seconds. If the speed of sound is 340 m/s, how far is the cliff?",[("The sound travels to the cliff and back, so total distance is 340 x 2 = 680 m.","Total path."),("The cliff is half of that distance: 680 / 2 = 340 m.","d = vt/2.")]),
("State the three frequency categories of sound and explain how an echo is formed.",["Audible 20 Hz to 20 kHz","Infrasonic below 20 Hz","Ultrasonic above 20 kHz","Echo from reflection with delay at least 0.1 s"],["Saying ultrasonic means very loud"]),
["Sound needs a medium and is produced by vibrations.","Audible 20 Hz-20 kHz; infrasonic and ultrasonic outside it.","v = f lambda; echo distance d = vt/2.","Doppler effect: apparent change in frequency."],
["n|Speed of sound in air (m/s) when f = 500 Hz and wavelength = 0.68 m|340|v = f lambda.",
 "t|Sound can travel through a vacuum.|False|It needs a material medium.",
 "m|Waves below 20 Hz are called|infrasonic|ultrasonic~Above 20 kHz|audible~Between 20 Hz and 20 kHz|Produced by earthquakes.",
 "n|Distance of cliff (m) if echo returns in 2 s and v = 340 m/s|340|d = vt/2.",
 "t|Bats can detect ultrasonic waves.|True|Frequencies above 20 kHz.",
 "s|The apparent change in frequency due to relative motion is the ____ effect.|Doppler|Named after Christian Doppler.",
 "m|An echo is heard separately when the reflected sound arrives after at least|0.1 s|0.01 s~Too short|1 minute~Too long|The ear keeps an impression for about 0.1 s.",
 "t|Sound travels faster in solids than in air.|True|Particles are closer in solids."])
